# T167/T169 (2026-08-25) — two prod-only defects fixed in this block:
#  1. Permissions-Policy denied camera + microphone SITE-WIDE, which kills the voice journal's
#     mic and the logbook's photo capture + QR scan in production (both shipped features).
#     (self) allows our own pages while still denying embedded third-party frames.
#     ★2026-08-28: geolocation=() was the SAME BUG, left behind by that same fix — a THIRD
#     shipped feature denied. marketplace-seller.html publishes a provider's live location during
#     an ACTIVE service job (watchPosition, one write per 15s, a visible "Sharing your live
#     location with this job's client" indicator, stopping when the job set empties). An empty
#     allowlist denies every origin INCLUDING self, so in production it never started — and it
#     failed invisibly, because its own error callback HIDES the indicator, making a blocked
#     permission look exactly like "no active job" while the waiting client saw nothing.
#     Fixing camera and microphone without auditing the third feature in the same header is how
#     a policy repeats itself; that is why csp-covers-what-ships now checks this line too.
#  2. The CSP predated GA4: script-src lacked googletagmanager.com and connect-src lacked the
#     analytics collection endpoints, so the deliberately-shipped GA4 (wh-ga4.js, funnel events,
#     anonymize_ip) was silently DEAD in prod — an analytics claim with no living producer.
#
# T169 (2026-08-28) — the SAME class again, this time LATENT and armed to fire on a config change.
# index.html ships a complete Cloudflare Turnstile integration behind a "configure-to-enable"
# switch documented as two steps: set window.WH_TURNSTILE_SITEKEY, then enable Bot Protection in
# the Supabase Auth dashboard. There was a silent THIRD step here, in a file nobody flipping that
# switch would think to open: script-src did not permit challenges.cloudflare.com, and no frame-src
# was declared at all — so it fell back to default-src 'self' and would have blocked the challenge
# iframe. Enabling bot defense would therefore have taken SIGNUP DOWN COMPLETELY: the script blocked
# => window.turnstile never defined => _turnstileToken() returns null => no captchaToken sent, while
# step two of that very switch tells Supabase to reject exactly that. An outage on the front door,
# caused by the feature meant to protect it, with a console-only cause and nothing local to
# reproduce it. The two directives below are Cloudflare's documented requirement; granted now, while
# the feature is off, so the documented switch is TRUE when someone finally throws it.
#
# T169 follow-up, same day — SWEEPING THE CLASS RATHER THAN THE INSTANCE. The Turnstile find was
# the third prod-only CSP failure recorded in this file, so instead of patching it and moving on,
# every external host referenced by shipped code was enumerated and checked against this policy.
# FOUR MORE SHIPPED FEATURES WERE SILENTLY BLOCKED IN PRODUCTION:
#   analytics.html          loads Plotly from cdn.plot.ly            -> charts never render
#   engineering-design.html loads html2pdf from cdnjs.cloudflare.com -> PDF export dead
#   engineering-design.js   calls engineering-calc-api.onrender.com  -> PDF + diagram APIs dead
#   marketplace.html        loads wh-map.js (MapLibre + OpenFreeMap) -> map dead twice over:
#                           tiles.openfreemap.org was missing from connect-src, AND MapLibre
#                           builds its renderer in a Worker from a blob: URL, which needs
#                           worker-src — absent entirely, so it inherited default-src 'self'.
# ★THE MAP CASE IS THE ONE TO REMEMBER: wh-map.js states its requirement in its own header
# ("Prod CSP needs connect-src https://tiles.openfreemap.org + worker-src blob:"). The requirement
# was written down, by the person who knew it, in the file that needed it — and never reached the
# file that ENFORCES it. A note in the consumer is not a change in the policy. That is the same
# shape as the Turnstile switch above, and it is why the class now has a gate
# (tools/validate_csp_covers_what_ships.py) instead of a fourth comment.
/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-XSS-Protection: 1; mode=block
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(self), geolocation=(self)
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://cdn.tailwindcss.com https://cdn.jsdelivr.net https://fonts.googleapis.com https://www.googletagmanager.com https://challenges.cloudflare.com https://cdn.plot.ly https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data: https: https://www.googletagmanager.com; connect-src 'self' https://hzyvnjtisfgbksicrouu.supabase.co https://workhive-assistant.ian-beronio37.workers.dev https://www.google-analytics.com https://analytics.google.com https://www.googletagmanager.com https://tiles.openfreemap.org https://engineering-calc-api.onrender.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; frame-src 'self' https://challenges.cloudflare.com; frame-ancestors 'none'

# Stale backups + the v3 test page are served from the web root (publish = "."), and
# each is a near-duplicate of the live homepage or logbook with the same <title>, no
# canonical and no robots meta. robots.txt disallows them, but that is a request, not
# an enforcement: a crawler that ignores it — or a stray inbound link — can put a
# duplicate of our own front page into an index we are trying to win. X-Robots-Tag is
# served with the response, so it applies whether or not the HTML is parsed.
# These files should ultimately not be deployed at all; this is the safe interim.
/index.backup.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
/index.backup2.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
/checklist.backup.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
/logbook.backup.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
/index-v3-test.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# Netlify publishes the repo root verbatim (publish = "."), so every gate baseline,
# audit report and platform-check log in the root is reachable at workhiveph.com/<name>
# — ~276 files, several over 70KB, whose text narrates internal architecture, table
# names and security findings. These are not blocked at the edge because the internal
# dashboards legitimately read a few (design-system.html reads *_baseline.json), so
# this marks them unindexable rather than unreachable. robots.txt carries matching
# Disallow rules. The real fix is excluding them from the publish step.
/*.log
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
/*_baseline.json
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
/*_report.json
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
/*-output.txt
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# Markdown twins (llms.txt convention): every page also exists at <page>.md as clean
# markdown — 2.66MB of HTML across the site becomes 845KB, with no nav, no inline CSS
# and no scripts for an agent to parse around. Served as text/markdown so a fetch gets
# text rather than a download prompt.
#
# noindex is deliberate: these are an ALTERNATE REPRESENTATION of pages that are
# already indexed, so leaving them indexable would enter 119 near-duplicates into
# classic search competing with the canonical HTML. noindex does not stop an agent
# fetching them, which is the entire point of the convention.
/*.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

# Cache policy (Maturity Phase 2 — CA). HTML + the service worker must always
# revalidate so a deploy is picked up immediately; the SW's CACHE_NAME bump is
# the shell-invalidation mechanism. Static immutable media caches long.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/*.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
/*.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
# 2026-08-28 (T160): .jpg was missing from this list while .png/.svg/.webp were on it — invisible
# until the companion portraits moved from 2 MB PNGs to 9.6 KB progressive JPEGs and quietly lost a
# year of caching in the process. A format swap inherits the cache policy of its NEW extension, not
# the old one. (No rule for *.js is deliberate and stays: these scripts are not content-hashed, so
# immutable caching would make a deploy unpickupable — they must revalidate.)
/*.jpg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/*.jpeg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
# The gap was wider than the two files that exposed it: 51 tracked .jpeg assets had never been
# covered either, and .ico is fetched on EVERY page load. Enumerated from `git ls-files` rather than
# from the extensions I happened to be thinking about.
/*.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/*.mp4
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/*.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/*.webp
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/*.woff2
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
